\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:21:  N->>A: thread/start\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:24:  N->>A: turn/start (FLOW_EFFORT)\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:26:  N-->>C: Signal Started\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:32:restart is authorized only when the supplied Flow ID and harness session equal\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:37:If `thread/start` succeeds but the first turn fails, a durable Pending record\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:38:keeps the known thread. A later child-self restart resumes it rather than\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:39:launching a second thread. A thread-start failure leaves a non-resumable\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:44:lifecycle. Message Nexus consumes that typed reply instead of maintaining a\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:49:the request names the caller. Herdr's snapshot carries no process IDs, so the\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:50:pane is read where Herdr marks it: every process Herdr starts in a pane\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:51:inherits `HERDR_SESSION` and `HERDR_PANE_ID`, and the Nexus reads them from\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:58:Start registers one. The optional FlowId in the request is the caller's claim:\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:63:flow is that binding (thread, harness, Herdr session, pane and terminal) and\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:66:never a gate: routing and promotion follow the Herdr route. `ResolvePeer`\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:67:on the meta socket answers the same question for a process Message names.\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:DESIGN.md:70:continuation act only on a route that still matches the native Herdr\\nbc464e5e1b94fcc179af73111f43b69db1f69fc5:D